{"ts": "2026-08-17T11:28:24Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 120, "kind": "tool_result", "text": "1\tNo new commits yet (all work is uncommitted), so there's no commit-message convention to check at this stage.\n2\t\n3\t## Review: Taiga #5 \u2014 Keep tasks when the server restarts (attempt 2)\n4\t\n5\t**Will block**\n6\t\n7\t- **`.gitignore` still missing `backend/data/`** (`/home/dev/projects/test-project/.gitignore`, 4 lines, unchanged by this diff). `docs/spec.md`'s own \"Affected areas\" section requires this line specifically so persisted task data is never committed. It's still absent \u2014 confirmed by reading the file directly. The moment someone runs the real server locally (`uvicorn app.main:app --reload`) and later does a broad `git add`, `backend/data/tasks.json` \u2014 containing real user-entered task titles \u2014 becomes trackable. The build handoff says this was blocked by session write permissions on that specific filename and needs a human to add it by hand; that's understood, but from a spec-coverage standpoint the deliverable isn't done, so this stays a blocker until the line actually lands, not a note for later.\n8\t\n9\t**Will comment**\n10\t\n11\t- `backend/app/store.py:35-40` \u2014 `_load`'s `except Exception:` catch logs only the file path, never the exception itself (`logger.warning(\"Could not parse %s; ...\", self._data_file)` \u2014 no `%s` for `str(exc)`, no `exc_info=True`). Alan's lens: a failure path should log enough to diagnose *what* went wrong, not just *that* something did. As written, a permission error, a `JSONDecodeError`, and a Pydantic validation error are all indistinguishable in the log, which will slow down whoever has to debug a real corrupt-file incident later. Cheap fix whenever this file is next touched: `logger.warning(\"Could not parse %s (%s); starting with an empty task list.\", self._data_file, exc)`.\n12\t\n13\t**Worth considering**\n14\t\n15\t- Same-process concurrent writers: FastAPI's sync `def` routes run in Starlette's threadpool, so two in-flight requests can call `_save()` concurrently and race on the shared `.tmp` file / `os.replace`. This was already raised and explicitly deferred as a follow-up in the prior round (noted in this round's build handoff as \"#4, not addressed\"), and the spec's non-goal (\"no multi-process/multi-worker locking... this app runs as a single process\") only strictly rules out multi-*process* races, not multi-*thread* ones \u2014 worth a sentence in a follow-up ticket rather than blocking this cycle on it.\n16\t\n17\t**Verified:** `python3 -m pytest -q` \u2192 30 passed (same run I did in this review). Load/save round-trip, missing/corrupt/wrong-shape/empty-file handling, id-continuation after reload, and directory auto-creation are all covered by `backend/tests/test_persistence.py` and match spec ACs 1\u20138. Reviewed disk state before/after \u2014 nothing left in the tree by this review pass.\n18\t\n19\tVERDICT: changes-requested", "meta": {"native_type": "user", "tool_use_id": "toolu_019moWAtJWdd4j1Ymx8syn3s", "session_id": "ab249e80-4d7e-43b0-9f8b-5d4464bab442"}}
