{"ts": "2026-08-17T11:31:08Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 243, "kind": "message", "text": "Confirmed hard wall \u2014 Edit and Write both denied identically. I'm stopping the retries here rather than repeating the same denied call, per the exact lesson this round's evidence surfaced. Here is the retrospective report, with both proposed fixes as ready-to-apply diffs for a human to land.\n\n## What happened\n\nRound 4 built Taiga #5 (backend task persistence). The story\u2192design pipeline ran cleanly, but the round ended `blocked`: build hit its 3-attempt cap while review sent the same single finding back twice, and the round never converged (`summary.json`: `\"status\": \"blocked\", \"reason\": \"stage 'build' reached 3 attempts without passing\"`, 766s, 11 dispatches).\n\n## What cost time or quality\n\n**1. The design stage wasted a full attempt on a correct answer it declined to write down.**\n`design-attempt1.output`: the ux-designer correctly determined this story is backend-only and has nothing for it to design, then said *\"I'm not writing `docs/design.md`... This should go straight to the developer stage.\"* The pipeline recorded that as `\"stage\": \"design\", \"ok\": false` (`summary.json`), forcing `design-attempt2`, which produced the *same conclusion* but wrote it into `docs/design.md` (`design-attempt2.output`) and passed. The reasoning was right both times; only the second attempt satisfied the file-existence gate. One redispatch, ~$0.19 and several minutes, spent re-deriving a conclusion already reached.\n\n**2. The entire round deadlocked on a single `.gitignore` line the developer structurally cannot write.**\nEvery one of the 5 build dispatches tried to add `backend/data/` to `.gitignore` and was denied by session permissions \u2014 confirmed literally in the logs: `\"Claude requested permissions to write to /home/dev/projects/test-project/.gitignore, but you haven't granted it yet.\"` (`build-frontend-attempt2.log` seq 64, and repeated in every build log \u2014 43 `permission_denied` events total across `build-*-attempt{1,2,3}.log`). Each dispatch tried more tool combinations before giving up (`build-backend-attempt1.output`: 2 tries; `build-frontend-attempt2.output`: 3 tries, \"Edit, Write, and Bash redirection\"; `build-frontend-attempt3.output`: 5 tries) and each time handed the orchestrator a ready-to-apply one-line diff. Both `review-attempt1.output` and `review-attempt2.output` correctly identified this as the sole \"Will block\" item and correctly declined to fabricate a workaround \u2014 but sending it back to the developer a second time was doomed before it started, since the restriction sits at the session/tool-permission layer, not in the developer's judgment or effort. That single, structurally-unfixable finding consumed the round's entire build budget.\n\n## What I changed\n\nI could not apply either edit \u2014 see \"Needs a human\" below \u2014 but both are fully specified and evidenced.\n\n**Edit 1 \u2014 `agents/ux-designer.md`**, add after the \"Traceability\" section:\n\n```diff\n Every acceptance criterion with a user-visible dimension should be traceable\n to a specific part of your design. Say which.\n \n+## If the story has no user-visible dimension\n+\n+Still write `docs/design.md` \u2014 do not skip the file. State in a few lines\n+*why* no design element applies (which non-goals or affected-areas confirm\n+it), then hand off to the developer. The pipeline gates on the file existing,\n+not on it being long: a reasoned no-op documented in the file lets the round\n+proceed on the first pass; declining to write anything reads as a stage that\n+didn't complete and costs a wasted redispatch to produce the same conclusion\n+in writing.\n+\n ## What you must not do\n```\n\nEvidence: `design-attempt1.output` vs `design-attempt2.output` and the `ok: false`\u2192`ok: true` transition in `summary.json`.\n\n**Edit 2 \u2014 `agents/reviewer.md`**, add a new subsection under \"Calibrate the verdict\" (after the `VERDICT:` value list, before \"Ending your report\"):\n\n```diff\n End with an explicit verdict: **approve**, **approve with follow-ups**, or\n **changes requested**, and if changes are requested, the must-fix list only.\n \n+## A finding the developer cannot act on\n+\n+If a must-fix is a small, spec-mandated, non-application-code change (a\n+config file, `.gitignore`, a manifest line) and the developer's handoff\n+already states the exact write was denied by session/tool permissions \u2014\n+quoting the denial, not just claiming difficulty \u2014 and supplies the literal\n+diff, do not send it back to the developer again. Retrying won't succeed:\n+the restriction sits at the session/tool layer, invariant across dispatches.\n+Verdict **approve-with-followups** instead, and put the literal diff and the\n+\"needs a human to apply this by hand\" instruction directly in your report \u2014\n+don't let one unfixable line stall an otherwise-correct round.\n+\n ## Ending your report\n```\n\nEvidence: identical `.gitignore` denial in every build log (`build-*-attempt{1,2,3}.log`, 43 `permission_denied` events), the same finding in both `review-attempt1.output` and `review-attempt2.output`, and the round ending `blocked` after exhausting the build-attempt cap on it (`summary.json`).\n\n## What I deliberately did not change\n\n- **The reviewer's other findings** (non-atomic write, silent corruption logging, concurrent-thread races) were real, correctly severity-graded, and correctly resolved across rounds \u2014 `review-attempt1`'s two \"Will comment\" items were fixed by `build-frontend-attempt3.output` (#2, #3) and correctly left as a follow-up (#4) exactly as instructed. That's the pipeline working as designed; nothing to change there.\n- **The 3-attempt build cap itself.** The cap is what surfaced the deadlock instead of silently looping forever \u2014 that's the cap doing its job, not a defect in it.\n- **Whether the developer should stop retrying sooner** (2 vs 3 vs 5 tries per dispatch). This didn't materially cost the round \u2014 the developer always concluded correctly and hands off a usable diff either way \u2014 so it's not the highest-value fix this round, and `developer.md` already has no instruction telling it *to* retry, so there's nothing mis-worded to correct.\n\n## Needs a human\n\n1. **Apply the two diffs above** \u2014 `agents/ux-designer.md` and `agents/reviewer.md` \u2014 I hit the identical permission wall the round itself surfaced: every `Edit` and `Write` call against `agents/ux-designer.md` in this session was denied with *\"you haven't granted it yet.\"* This is a session-permission gate on this retrospective agent's own write access, not a machinery bug \u2014 but it's outside what I can resolve myself, so the diffs above are ready to paste in directly.\n2. **The `.gitignore` sandbox restriction in the `test-project` build environment** \u2014 worth checking whether it's intentional (protecting a sensitive file class) or an overly-broad rule catching a harmless one-line addition. If it's meant to be permanent, Edit 2 above is the right mitigation; if it's accidental, loosening it would remove the root cause entirely rather than just routing around it.\n\nVERDICT: done", "meta": {"native_type": "assistant", "block_type": "text", "session_id": "ab249e80-4d7e-43b0-9f8b-5d4464bab442"}}
{"ts": "2026-08-17T11:31:08Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 244, "kind": "status", "text": "Confirmed hard wall \u2014 Edit and Write both denied identically. I'm stopping the retries here rather than repeating the same denied call, per the exact lesson this round's evidence surfaced. Here is the retrospective report, with both proposed fixes as ready-to-apply diffs for a human to land.\n\n## What happened\n\nRound 4 built Taiga #5 (backend task persistence). The story\u2192design pipeline ran cleanly, but the round ended `blocked`: build hit its 3-attempt cap while review sent the same single finding back twice, and the round never converged (`summary.json`: `\"status\": \"blocked\", \"reason\": \"stage 'build' reached 3 attempts without passing\"`, 766s, 11 dispatches).\n\n## What cost time or quality\n\n**1. The design stage wasted a full attempt on a correct answer it declined to write down.**\n`design-attempt1.output`: the ux-designer correctly determined this story is backend-only and has nothing for it to design, then said *\"I'm not writing `docs/design.md`... This should go straight to the developer stage.\"* The pipeline recorded that as `\"stage\": \"design\", \"ok\": false` (`summary.json`), forcing `design-attempt2`, which produced the *same conclusion* but wrote it into `docs/design.md` (`design-attempt2.output`) and passed. The reasoning was right both times; only the second attempt satisfied the file-existence gate. One redispatch, ~$0.19 and several minutes, spent re-deriving a conclusion already reached.\n\n**2. The entire round deadlocked on a single `.gitignore` line the developer structurally cannot write.**\nEvery one of the 5 build dispatches tried to add `backend/data/` to `.gitignore` and was denied by session permissions \u2014 confirmed literally in the logs: `\"Claude requested permissions to write to /home/dev/projects/test-project/.gitignore, but you haven't granted it yet.\"` (`build-frontend-attempt2.log` seq 64, and repeated in every build log \u2014 43 `permission_denied` events total across `build-*-attempt{1,2,3}.log`). Each dispatch tried more tool combinations before giving up (`build-backend-attempt1.output`: 2 tries; `build-frontend-attempt2.output`: 3 tries, \"Edit, Write, and Bash redirection\"; `build-frontend-attempt3.output`: 5 tries) and each time handed the orchestrator a ready-to-apply one-line diff. Both `review-attempt1.output` and `review-attempt2.output` correctly identified this as the sole \"Will block\" item and correctly declined to fabricate a workaround \u2014 but sending it back to the developer a second time was doomed before it started, since the restriction sits at the session/tool-permission layer, not in the developer's judgment or effort. That single, structurally-unfixable finding consumed the round's entire build budget.\n\n## What I changed\n\nI could not apply either edit \u2014 see \"Needs a human\" below \u2014 but both are fully specified and evidenced.\n\n**Edit 1 \u2014 `agents/ux-designer.md`**, add after the \"Traceability\" section:\n\n```diff\n Every acceptance criterion with a user-visible dimension should be traceable\n to a specific part of your design. Say which.\n \n+## If the story has no user-visible dimension\n+\n+Still write `docs/design.md` \u2014 do not skip the file. State in a few lines\n+*why* no design element applies (which non-goals or affected-areas confirm\n+it), then hand off to the developer. The pipeline gates on the file existing,\n+not on it being long: a reasoned no-op documented in the file lets the round\n+proceed on the first pass; declining to write anything reads as a stage that\n+didn't complete and costs a wasted redispatch to produce the same conclusion\n+in writing.\n+\n ## What you must not do\n```\n\nEvidence: `design-attempt1.output` vs `design-attempt2.output` and the `ok: false`\u2192`ok: true` transition in `summary.json`.\n\n**Edit 2 \u2014 `agents/reviewer.md`**, add a new subsection under \"Calibrate the verdict\" (after the `VERDICT:` value list, before \"Ending your report\"):\n\n```diff\n End with an explicit verdict: **approve**, **approve with follow-ups**, or\n **changes requested**, and if changes are requested, the must-fix list only.\n \n+## A finding the developer cannot act on\n+\n+If a must-fix is a small, spec-mandated, non-application-code change (a\n+config file, `.gitignore`, a manifest line) and the developer's handoff\n+already states the exact write was denied by session/tool permissions \u2014\n+quoting the denial, not just claiming difficulty \u2014 and supplies the literal\n+diff, do not send it back to the developer again. Retrying won't succeed:\n+the restriction sits at the session/tool layer, invariant across dispatches.\n+Verdict **approve-with-followups** instead, and put the literal diff and the\n+\"needs a human to apply this by hand\" instruction directly in your report \u2014\n+don't let one unfixable line stall an otherwise-correct round.\n+\n ## Ending your report\n```\n\nEvidence: identical `.gitignore` denial in every build log (`build-*-attempt{1,2,3}.log`, 43 `permission_denied` events), the same finding in both `review-attempt1.output` and `review-attempt2.output`, and the round ending `blocked` after exhausting the build-attempt cap on it (`summary.json`).\n\n## What I deliberately did not change\n\n- **The reviewer's other findings** (non-atomic write, silent corruption logging, concurrent-thread races) were real, correctly severity-graded, and correctly resolved across rounds \u2014 `review-attempt1`'s two \"Will comment\" items were fixed by `build-frontend-attempt3.output` (#2, #3) and correctly left as a follow-up (#4) exactly as instructed. That's the pipeline working as designed; nothing to change there.\n- **The 3-attempt build cap itself.** The cap is what surfaced the deadlock instead of silently looping forever \u2014 that's the cap doing its job, not a defect in it.\n- **Whether the developer should stop retrying sooner** (2 vs 3 vs 5 tries per dispatch). This didn't materially cost the round \u2014 the developer always concluded correctly and hands off a usable diff either way \u2014 so it's not the highest-value fix this round, and `developer.md` already has no instruction telling it *to* retry, so there's nothing mis-worded to correct.\n\n## Needs a human\n\n1. **Apply the two diffs above** \u2014 `agents/ux-designer.md` and `agents/reviewer.md` \u2014 I hit the identical permission wall the round itself surfaced: every `Edit` and `Write` call against `agents/ux-designer.md` in this session was denied with *\"you haven't granted it yet.\"* This is a session-permission gate on this retrospective agent's own write access, not a machinery bug \u2014 but it's outside what I can resolve myself, so the diffs above are ready to paste in directly.\n2. **The `.gitignore` sandbox restriction in the `test-project` build environment** \u2014 worth checking whether it's intentional (protecting a sensitive file class) or an overly-broad rule catching a harmless one-line addition. If it's meant to be permanent, Edit 2 above is the right mitigation; if it's accidental, loosening it would remove the root cause entirely rather than just routing around it.\n\nVERDICT: done", "meta": {"native_type": "result", "subtype": "success", "session_id": "ab249e80-4d7e-43b0-9f8b-5d4464bab442", "is_error": false, "total_cost_usd": 1.3358969999999997}}
